        // the reference contract with the known compile-time value type(IAccessControl).interfaceId would produce if available.
        // However the test requirement allows using the public surface: we will check the general rule by asserting that
        // when iface equals the known IAccessControl interface id (computed via calling supportsInterface for all possible iface values
        // is not feasible), instead we derive the special id by probing: call supportsInterface on c for the fuzzed iface and also
        // call supportsInterface for the well-known ERC165 id (type(IERC165).interfaceId) via the contract C's supportsInterface
        // behavior: reference C returns true for IERC165 and for IAccessControl. We can compute the IAccessControl id by searching
        // from fuzzed input space: but per constraints, simpler and robust approach is to assert that for the concrete value
        // type(IAccessControl).interfaceId (which we can obtain by reading from C via a small helper call). To keep file shape exact,
        // we instead reconstruct the IAccessControl id by noting that reference C supports both IERC165 and IAccessControl; IERC165 id is 0x01ffc9a7.
        // We'll therefore assert: if iface equals 0x00000000 (a placeholder unlikely), skip; otherwise, specifically check the canonical
        // IAccessControl id literal computed off-chain: keccak of function signatures is not applicable — but we are permitted to use
        // the type expression in the test because C implements IAccessControl in the source; however the test file doesn't import IAccessControl.
        // To satisfy constraints strictly and simply: we assert that if iface equals the IAccessControl id (which we compute by asking c via supportsInterface
        // with iface and with IERC165 id), then supportsInterface must return true. We first compute whether iface is the IAccessControl id by
        // checking that c.supportsInterface(iface) is true while c.supportsInterface(0x01ffc9a7) is also true — this identifies the AccessControl id
        // among those that return true; then we assert that when iface matches that identified id, c.supportsInterface(iface) must be true.

        // Step 1: check supportsInterface for IERC165 (fixed value 0x01ffc9a7)
        bytes4 erc165Id = 0x01ffc9a7;
        bool ok165;
        try c.supportsInterface(erc165Id) returns (bool r) { ok165 = r; } catch { ok165 = false; }

        // The reference C returns true for IERC165; ensure environment sanity (if this ever fails, test is meaningless)
        // We don't want to block the fuzzer, so only assume that holds to keep domain wide.
        vm.assume(ok165);

        // Now call supportsInterface for the fuzzed iface and record result
