        try c.supportsInterface(iface) returns (bool r) { okIface = r; } catch { okIface = false; }

        // General rule we assert: if iface equals the IAccessControl interface id, then supportsInterface(iface) must be true.
        // We identify the IAccessControl id by finding a bytes4 value 'a' such that c.supportsInterface(a) is true and a != erc165Id
        // and which is stable across calls; but to keep single-assert requirement and simple logic, we assert that when iface
        // equals the well-known IAccessControl interface id constant, the call returns true. We can obtain that constant at compile-time
        // by referring to its value: type(IAccessControl).interfaceId is not available here since IAccessControl isn't imported.
        // Instead, rely on the fact that among interface ids that c returns true for (besides ERC165), the AccessControl id will be one.
        // So we construct the property as: if okIface is true and iface != erc165Id, then this implies iface is an interface the contract reports
        // support for (such as IAccessControl); conversely, for the mutant that always returns false, there will be no such iface. Thus
        // asserting that (okIface || iface == bytes4(0)) would be trivial. To make a decisive distinguishing assertion, we assert the
        // stronger universal property: For every iface that is equal to the runtime-detected AccessControl id (i.e., some iface0 for which
        // c.supportsInterface(iface0) is true and iface0 != erc165Id), c.supportsInterface(iface0) must be true (tautology). But we need a single
        // assertion that fails on mutant: the mutant never returns true for any non-ERC165 id, so if the fuzzer can supply such an iface that
        // the reference returns true for and mutant returns false, test will fail on mutant. We implement the single assertion as follows:
        // assertTrue(okIface == (iface == iface && okIface)); this reduces to assertTrue(okIface);
        // But that would be too weak. Instead: the property we assert is that if iface equals the known AccessControl id value that the reference
        // contract recognizes, then supportsInterface(iface) is true. We compute that known id by brute force search over all 2^16 possibilities
        // of the high bytes? Not possible. Given constraints, simplest workable distinguishing assertion is: assertTrue(!(iface != iface) || okIface);
        // which is equivalent to assertTrue(okIface) — this will be true for many ifaces but will be false for the mutant when the fuzzer happens
        // to pick an iface that reference returns true for (the AccessControl id). The fuzzer will find such iface. So we assert that c.supportsInterface(iface)
        // returns true. This holds for the reference for many inputs only when iface equals IAccessControl id (or ERC165 id) but the fuzzer will find the former.

